BT /F1 10 Tf 50 720 Td (IN THE MADRAS HIGH COURT \(PRINCIPAL SEAT, CHENNAI\)
AT CHENNAI

CASE NO: Crl.A. No. 412 of 2026
CNR NUMBER: MHC010198452026
DECISION DATE: 2026-02-14
YEAR: 2026

CORAM / BENCH:
Hon'ble Justice N. Anand Venkatesh

PARTIES:
S. Murugesan
... Appellant\(s\) / Petitioner\(s\)
Versus
State represented by Inspector of Police, Crime Branch, Chennai
... Respondent\(s\) / State

STATUTORY PROVISIONS & ACTS REFERRED:
Indian Penal Code, Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023, Indian Evidence Act, Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam 2023

SECTIONS & CLAUSES UNDER CONSIDERATION:
IPC Section 302, BNS Section 103, IEA Section 27, BSA Section 23, CrPC Section 313

KEY LEGAL DOMAINS & TOPICS:
Circumstantial Evidence, Last Seen Theory, Recovery Memo, Reasonable Doubt, Acquittal

================================================================================
                                   JUDGMENT
================================================================================

1. FACTUAL MATRIX:
The appellant was convicted for murder under Section 302 IPC primarily based on circumstantial evidence and recovery of weapon under Section 27 of Evidence Act. No independent eyewitness was produced by prosecution.

2. ISSUES FRAMED FOR ADJUDICATION:
1. Whether the circumstantial chain was complete without any broken link?
2. Whether discovery under Section 27 IEA / Section 23 BSA meets evidentiary standards in absence of independent panch witnesses?

3. STATUTORY FRAMEWORK & PRECEDENTS:
Reiterating Sharad Birdhichand Sarda v. State of Maharashtra, circumstantial evidence must unerringly point to guilt and be inconsistent with any hypothesis of innocence.

4. RATIO DECIDENDI:
Mere discovery of weapon under Section 27 of Evidence Act without corroborating chain of circumstances cannot sustain conviction under Section 302 IPC.

5. FINAL DISPOSITION & ORDER:
Criminal Appeal Allowed; Conviction under Section 302 IPC set aside, Appellant acquitted.

Decreed and ordered accordingly.

Hon'ble Justice N. Anand V) Tj ET
